#: lib/potok_ide_web/controllers/page_html.ex:245
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "A group's description doesn't have to be just text. It can also be a small app, say a form, a poll or a task list. Or pick a template, say news or an event, and fill in the fields."
msgstr ""

#: lib/potok_ide_web/controllers/page_html.ex:200
#, elixir-autogen, elixir-format
msgid "Branch out your groups as far as you need. Each one is public or private, a sub-group can inherit members and roles, and unread counts add up through the whole tree. You can react to a group just as you do to a message. You start a new group from a ready setup: chat, folder, announcements, feed, polls, form, map or page. You can then narrow the list of sub-groups by kind, to the groups you are in, to those you created, or to those you follow. You can put a group on a map, with a pin you pick for it, and the group's kind stays in the pin's corner. The map of groups shows every group with a place that you can see, and you narrow it with the same filters, by time and by pin. A map in a group can also hold tens of thousands of places that an app brings in from a list, such as the rows of an open dataset: they stay on that map, and the map of groups leaves them out. You can also draw a group an area, like a local community's, and the map then shows what lies in it and which areas a place lies in. You can also give a group a route, such as a walk, a race or a bike path: draw it or bring it from a GPX, KML or GeoJSON file, and the map shows where it goes and which areas it passes through. A search finds a group even when you type its name without diacritics, in Latin or in Cyrillic. From a group's page, one tap shows it on the map of groups. You can follow a group you see even when you are not its member, and its notices then come to you apart from the messages, with their own counter. The groups you follow stay on the list and on the map of groups even while the other public groups are hidden."
msgstr ""

